# Cloud agents

Start every subagent with `environment` set to `cloud` and `run_in_background` set to true. That includes swarm workers, arena runners, architect runners, code delegates, and verifiers.

Pass `cloud_base_branch` when the subagent must start from a branch other than the repository default.

Each writer gets its own `cursor/` branch. Do not create a git worktree for that work.

Set `environment` to `local` only when the task needs this machine. That covers a simulator, local IDE state, a transcript that exists only on that machine, or a credential that exists only there.

Orchestrate's sub-coordinator stays `local`. It needs the coordinator's store, and a cloud agent cannot read that store. Workers that the sub-coordinator spawns still use `environment` `cloud`, unless the task needs this machine.

## Arena and architect

`/arena` and `/architect` use the same handoff. The parent sets each runner's `environment` to `cloud`. Each runner pushes its candidate to its own `cursor/` branch. The parent fetches those branches before judging or grafting. A git worktree is not the candidate output location.

Every build brief ends with this sentence.

`Run tools/verify.sh. After branch cursor/verify-golf-api-skill-ca4b is merged, use /verify-golf-api.`

# Models

This file is the model table for this repository. It overrides pstack skill default slugs, including any default that is not listed here. It also overrides a missing `~/.cursor/rules/pstack-models.mdc`. Cloud VMs do not have that home file. Use only the slugs in the table below. Do not pass a skill default slug to the Task tool.

Use Grok slugs only. Each model field holds one slug. A single-seat line is one slug. A panel line is a comma-separated list, and each entry is one slug. `arena cross-judge pool` is a list. Pick one slug from that pool, and pick a slug other than the model that wrote the change.

The builder slug is `grok-4.7-xhigh-fast`. Removing `-fast` leaves `grok-4.7-xhigh`. That slug is the same model as the builder. It is not a reviewer seat, and it is not in this table. Reviewer seats use a different slug.

`grok-4.5-high` is not a Task slug. The seat that used it now uses `cursor-grok-4.6-high`. The table writes `cursor-grok-4.6-xhigh`. The Task tool rewrites `grok-4.6-xhigh` to that listed slug.

If the Task tool rejects a slug, use the nearest slug from this table and say which one you used.

